= Identity — Technical =

== Classical identity ==
First-order logic with identity commonly includes:

<pre>
∀x (x = x)
</pre>

and substitution principles ensuring that identicals are indiscernible with respect to predicates expressible in the language.

Identity is normally interpreted as actual equality on the domain.

== Leibniz's law ==
One formulation:

<pre>
x = y → ∀P(Px ↔ Py)
</pre>

requires care because quantification over properties depends on the logical framework.

The converse identity-of-indiscernibles principle is not simply a theorem of first-order logic.

== Unique Name Assumption ==
Many databases/programming contexts behave as though different names imply different objects.

OWL does not generally adopt the Unique Name Assumption.

Therefore two distinct IRIs are not automatically inferred to denote distinct individuals.

Difference can be explicitly asserted.

== owl:sameAs ==
Under OWL semantics, <code>owl:sameAs</code> equates individual denotations.

Consequences associated with one can transfer to the other through equality reasoning.

Using it as a record-linking confidence relation is therefore semantically unsafe.

== Identity over versions ==
Versioned artifacts need an explicit identity policy.

Questions include:
* Is version 2 the same dataset or a new dataset?
* Is a fork the same project?
* Does a renamed organization preserve identity?
* Does replacement of all physical components preserve identity?

The correct answer depends on the type and purpose of identification.

== Entity resolution thresholding ==
Probabilistic match score:

<pre>
P(sameEntity | evidence)
</pre>

is not itself identity.

Thresholding converts uncertainty into an operational decision and can create false merges/splits.

Systems should retain provenance and confidence where possible.

